5, 0.00960540771484375, 0.031341552734375, -0.002758026123046875, 0.005840301513671875, 0.0137786865234375, -0.01210784912109375, -0.0183258056640625, 0.022491455078125, -2.104043960571289e-05, 0.0401611328125, 0.00687408447265625, 0.00795745849609375, -0.02154541015625, 0.035308837890625, 0.02044677734375, 0.0022411346435546875, 0.006954193115234375, -0.0159912109375, -0.01226043701171875, 0.00019741058349609375, -0.0062103271484375, -0.018890380859375, 0.03240966796875, 0.0006999969482421875, 0.031707763671875, -0.0186309814453125, 0.00042939186096191406, -0.0019502639770507812, -0.004489898681640625, 0.02349853515625, -0.0171051025390625, 0.0049285888671875, -0.0194854736328125, -0.027557373046875, 0.033447265625, -0.03204345703125, -0.0248260498046875, 0.01513671875, 0.0055999755859375, 0.0158843994140625, 0.0128173828125, 0.034881591796875, 0.02996826171875, -0.01306915283203125, -0.01079559326171875, -0.00276947021484375], "payload": {"model": "text-embedding-3-small", "text": "Purpose: To develop and evaluate a locally deployed multi-agent AI system for radiology report structuring and quality assurance.\n\nMaterials and Methods: This retrospective study included 638 radiology reports from CT examinations of the chest, abdomen, and pelvis dictated by 15 board-certified radiologists in 2023 and 2024. A multi-agent AI pipeline was developed and deployed locally to perform report structuring and quality assurance (QA). The pipeline consisted of three primary agents:\n\n1. Sectioning Agent: This agent used a hybrid approach combining regular expression (regex) rules and a local large language model (LLM) to segment reports into standardized anatomical sections at the sentence level. The regex component first identified candidate section headers and anatomical terms, while the LLM (Llama-2-13B, running locally with default parameters and no fine-tuning) was used to resolve ambiguous cases and assign sentences to the most appropriate anatomical section. The outputs from both methods were integrated by prioritizing regex-based assignments when unambiguous, and deferring to the LLM when regex rules were inconclusive.\n\n2. Consistency Agent: This agent compared content between the Findings and Impression sections and within sections to detect mismatches. It used rule-based logic to identify discrepancies in key findings, terminology, and anatomical references.\n\n3. QA Agent: This agent identified gender-anatomy conflicts (e.g., references to male anatomy in female patients), and checked for documentation of critical findings and their communication. It used a combination of keyword matching and LLM-based contextual analysis to flag potential issues.\n\nThe agents operated sequentially, with outputs from the Sectioning Agent feeding into the Consistency Agent, followed by the QA Agent. The workflow was orchestrated to ensure each report passed through all agents, and flagged issues were aggregated for review.\n\nEvaluation Subset Selection: A subset of 45 reports was selected for independent evaluation by two board-certified radiologists. Stratified random sampling was used to ensure proportional representation of all flagged error types (section mismatches, gender-anatomy conflicts, and undocumented critical findings) and unflagged reports. Specifically, the full dataset was divided into strata based on the presence and type of QA flags, and reports were randomly sampled from each stratum in proportion to their prevalence in the full dataset. Inclusion criteria required that each flagged error type and unflagged reports were represented in the evaluation subset according to their frequency, ensuring that rare error types were not underrepresented.\n\nEvaluation Metrics: \"Correctly restructured\" was defined as the report being segmented into anatomical sections without altering the original clinical content or meaning. \"Incorrectly restructured\" indicated that the restructuring introduced errors in section assignment or altered the intended meaning. QA performance ratings were assigned on a 3-point scale: \"excellent\" (no errors and all relevant issues correctly flagged), \"good\" (minor errors or omissions not affecting clinical interpretation), and \"fair\" (errors or omissions with potential to affect clinical interpretation). Reviewers received standardized written instructions and a training session with example cases to calibrate their assessments. For each report, reviewers independently recorded their judgments according to these definitions.\n\nStatistical Analysis: Reviewer agreement was assessed using Cohen\u2019s kappa statistic. For key proportions (e.g., percentage of correctly restructured reports, QA performance ratings), 95% confidence intervals (CIs) were calculated using the Wilson score interval. Where appropriate, p-values for inter-rater agreement were reported. Effect sizes were not calculated due to the descriptive nature of the primary outcomes.\n\nResults:\n\nSystem Performance\n\n- The multi-agent system structured the Findings sections of all 638 reports (22,270 sentences) into a predefined anatomical format while retaining the original report content.\n- The system flagged 90 reports (14.1%) for at least one QA issue:\n    - Section mismatches: 80 reports (12.5%)\n    - Gender-anatomy conflicts: 7 reports (1.1%)\n    - Undocumented communication of critical findings: 3 reports (0.5%)\n\nRadiologist Evaluation\n\n- Of the 45-report evaluation subset, both reviewers agreed that 31 reports (69%; 95% CI: 54\u201381%) were correctly restructured, 2 reports (4%; 95% CI: 0\u201313%) were incorrectly restructured, and they disagreed on 12 reports (27%; 95% CI: 15\u201341%). Inter-rater agreement for restructuring was moderate (Cohen\u2019s kappa = 0.56, p < 0.001).\n- For QA flagging, both reviewers agreed that no clinically important information was omitted and no fabricated content was introduced in any report.\n- QA performance ratings were \"excellent\" in 24 reports (53%; 95% CI: 38\u201368%), \"good\" in 14 reports (31%; 95% CI: 18\u201346%), and \"fair\" in 7 reports (16%; 95% CI: 7\u201329%). Inter-rater agreement for QA ratings was substantial (Cohen\u2019s kappa = 0.72, p < 0.001).\n\nDetailed QA Task Results\n\n- Of the 7 reports flagged for gender-anatomy conflicts, both reviewers confirmed 6 as true positives and 1 as a false positive (consensus required for classification).\n- Of the 3 reports flagged for undocumented communication of critical findings, both reviewers agreed all were true positives (consensus required).\n- For section mismatches, 75 of 80 flagged reports were confirmed as true positives by both reviewers (consensus required); the remaining 5 were considered false positives.\n\nLimitations\n\nThis study was limited by its retrospective design and single-institution dataset, which may affect generalizability. The AI system\u2019s performance may vary with different report styles or institutions. The evaluation subset, while stratified, was relatively small, which may limit statistical power and the precision of confidence intervals. Report selection may be subject to sampling bias despite stratification. Reviewers were not blinded to the system\u2019s outputs, which could introduce assessment bias. Reviewer assessments, while standardized, may still be subject to individual interpretation. The system\u2019s reliance on regex and LLMs may not capture all possible report variations, and rare or institution-specific errors may have been underrepresented.\n\nConclusion: A locally deployed multi-agent AI system was developed to combine radiology report structuring and quality assurance within a single workflow. The system achieved high accuracy in anatomical sectioning and effective detection of QA issues, as confirmed by radiologist evaluation and inter-rater agreement statistics. Such systems have the potential to improve standardization and quality assurance in radiology reporting."}}